# EPICQUIZ — UFC Epic Quiz: Product Requirements Document

**Status:** Draft v1
**Owner:** Giorgi (fnf.ge)
**Scope:** New UFC-section mini-game, built from the existing standalone prototype `ufc-epic-quiz.html`
**Author:** Claude Code (reviewed prototype at repo root, 2026-10-15)

---

## 1. Summary

Add a UFC trivia mini-game to fnf.ge's UFC section (`/ufc/`), with two game modes (**Quiz** — multiple choice, **Guess Who** — progressive-clue free-text) and two opponent modes (**vs AI**, **vs Player** — real-time 1v1). The feature already exists as a working, unintegrated prototype (`ufc-epic-quiz.html`, 1578 lines, repo root, untracked/orphaned — not linked from any page). This PRD covers what it takes to turn that prototype into a shipped, on-brand, safe-to-run feature.

**v1 is explicitly non-monetary** — no balance reads/writes, no coin rewards, no entry costs. It is a pure engagement/retention feature sitting next to (not inside) the betting flows that are production-critical elsewhere on this site.

---

## 2. Why (goals)

- Give UFC-section visitors something to do between fight nights / while a card is settling, to increase session frequency and time-on-site.
- Build a reusable "mini-game" pattern (question bank + admin CMS + matchmaking) that could extend to other sports later (not in scope now, but the data model shouldn't paint us into a UFC-only corner if the cost of generalizing is low).
- Validate engagement before ever considering tying it to balances/rewards (see §9 Non-Goals).

## 3. Current prototype — assessment

What's already good and should be kept:
- Questions/answers live server-side in Supabase (`quiz_questions`, `guess_who_questions`), not hardcoded in the page — avoids trivial view-source cheating.
- Clean difficulty-tier UX with a "locked" state for tiers that have no content yet.
- Georgian-aware fuzzy text matching for Guess Who (Levenshtein + word-level + surname-only fallback) — genuinely good work, keep as-is.
- Card-battle reveal sequence (both players' answers flip simultaneously, score bumps) is a strong, game-show-like payoff moment. Keep the mechanic.
- Already points at fnf.ge's real Supabase project (`qxfcwsiysnjxhxljqigl`) — not a separate backend to merge.

What must change before shipping:
1. **Visual design is a separate system from fnf.ge.** Different CSS variables, different display font (`Segoe UI Black` vs. site's `Anton`), slightly different red/gold values. Needs a full rebuild against `style.css` tokens (§5).
2. **"vs Player" is a single hardcoded global Realtime room** (`MP_ROOM = "ufc-quiz-room-1"`). Every visitor on the entire site who picks "vs human" joins the *same* channel — a third visitor joining mid-match becomes an orphaned spectator, and nothing stops multiple pairs from colliding. Not viable for real traffic; needs a proper matchmaking/room system (§7).
3. **Match results are computed and trusted client-side.** In human mode, each player locally computes `picks`/`points` and broadcasts them via Supabase Realtime (`finished` event) — the other client takes that payload at face value. A player can fabricate a perfect score from the browser console. Given this site's existing "server-authoritative" pattern for settlement (CLAUDE.md), scoring for PvP must move server-side (§7.4).
4. **No identity tied to the real account system.** Players type a free-text name per session; no link to `auth.users` / `users.nick`. No persistent stats are possible this way, and nothing stops name-spoofing.
5. **No content-ops path.** No admin UI exists to add/edit questions — would currently require hand-inserting Supabase rows.
6. **"AI opponent" is a weighted coin-flip**, not real evaluation (`Math.random() < accuracy`). That's a fine and standard trivia-game approach — just note in copy/PRD language it's a simulated difficulty curve, not a real model, so we don't over-promise.

---

## 4. Decisions locked for v1

| Area | Decision |
|---|---|
| Economy | **Free only.** No reads/writes of `users.balance` or `user_balances`. No coin rewards, no entry fees. Purely cosmetic/engagement rewards (leaderboard rank, badges) are allowed later but out of scope for v1. |
| PvP matchmaking | **Two options, both required:** (a) **Invite via auto-generated room/party code** — one player creates a room, shares a short code/link, the other joins it directly. (b) **Auto matchmaking** — a player taps "Find opponent"; if ≥2 players are searching concurrently they get paired at random server-side. Both replace the current single hardcoded room. |
| Content ops | **Admin CRUD tab in `admin/admin.html`** for `quiz_questions` and `guess_who_questions` (list / add / edit / delete / activate-deactivate), gated by the existing `is_admin` check — not raw Supabase table editing. |

---

## 5. Design integration

The prototype must be rebuilt against the site's actual design system rather than its own standalone one.

**Source of truth:** `style.css` (`:root` tokens) — `--red:#F31D25`, `--blue:#2E7BFF`, `--gold:#F5C451`, `--green:#3ad17e`, `--canvas:#0E0D14`, `--surface:#16141E`, `--line:#2A2738`, display font `Anton`, body font `Noto Sans Georgian`/`Archivo`. The prototype's own tokens (`--red:#e5202f`, font `Segoe UI Black`, etc.) get dropped entirely — no parallel design system.

**Entry point:** `ufc/index.html` has no existing "fun/community" section. Two placement options:
- **Recommended:** a new `<section id="quiz">` between `#tickets` (L175) and the existing `#socialJoin` promo block (L195) — same visual rhythm as the rest of the page (hero → card → tickets → quiz → social → leaderboard → how-it-works).
- Alternative: fold it into the `#socialJoin` area as a second promo card, if the page doesn't want another full section.

**Nav:** reuse the exact `fantasy-nav.js` injection pattern (it already DOM-injects a football flyout into `.nav-fx-pop` / `.mnav-pop` on every page, guarded by `window.__FNF_FANTASY_NAV__`). Add a "ქვიზი" link the same way — either as a flat top-level nav item (desktop `#navLinks` + mobile `.mnav`) scoped to UFC pages only (checking `location.pathname` for `/ufc/`), since this is a UFC-specific feature in v1.

**Script wiring:** follow the existing cache-busting convention — ship as `/ufc/quiz.js?v=1` (or similar), not inline in the HTML, so it can be bumped (`?v=2`, etc.) like `app.js?v=207` and `fantasy-nav.js?v=6` are today.

**Screens to rebuild (same structure, new skin):** game-type select → mode select (AI/Human) → lobby/matchmaking → quiz or guess-who gameplay → card-battle reveal → result. All of this logic (state machine, timers, clue reveal, fuzzy name matching) is reusable; only the DOM/CSS layer and the multiplayer/auth plumbing need rework.

---

## 6. Game modes (unchanged from prototype, confirmed as correct product design)

### 6.1 Quiz (multiple choice)
- `QUESTIONS_PER_GAME` per match (default 10, configurable, max 20).
- Per-question timer (default 15s); no answer = scored as wrong.
- Difficulty tiers (easy/medium/hard) map to a `level` column in `quiz_questions`; tiers with no content are shown locked (🔒), not hidden — keep this UX, it previews the roadmap to players.
- Scoring: 1 point per correct answer.

### 6.2 Guess Who (ვინ ვარ?)
- Progressive clues (1 shown initially, player can reveal more via "+ შემდეგი მინიშნება").
- Free-text Georgian answer, fuzzy-matched (Levenshtein + word-level + exact-substring surname fallback) — keep `isGuessCorrect()` logic as-is, it's well-tuned.
- Scoring inversely tied to clues used: 1 clue → 4 pts, 2 → 3, 3 → 2, 4+ → 1 pt (0 if wrong/timed out).
- vs AI: no timer, player-paced, "Next" button after each answer.
- vs Player: 25s timer per question, auto-advances.

### 6.3 Card Battle reveal (shared by both modes)
- After both players (or player + AI) finish all questions, answers are replayed one-by-one as a visual "card flip" — both sides' picks are revealed simultaneously with correct/wrong styling, score bumps animate, final screen shows win/lose/draw.
- Keep this mechanic; it's the emotional payoff of the whole feature and works well as designed.

---

## 7. vs Player (PvP) — real design, replacing the hardcoded room

This is the biggest rework relative to the prototype.

### 7.1 Data model (new Supabase tables)

- **`quiz_rooms`**
  `id (uuid, pk)`, `code (text, unique, short e.g. 6 chars)`, `game_type (quiz|guess)`, `level (text)`, `status (waiting|active|finished|abandoned)`, `host_user_id`, `guest_user_id`, `created_at`, `started_at`, `questions (jsonb, server-picked, never sent to client until match start)`.
- **`quiz_room_answers`**
  `room_id`, `user_id`, `question_index`, `pick (jsonb — index or free text)`, `answered_at`, `points (int, server-computed)`. One row per player per question — written via RPC only, never a direct client insert of a final score.
- **`quiz_queue`** (for auto matchmaking)
  `user_id`, `game_type`, `level`, `queued_at`. A player "searching" is a row here; matching pulls two queued rows for the same `(game_type, level)` and atomically creates a `quiz_rooms` row for both, deleting the queue rows.

### 7.2 Invite / party code flow
- Player A taps "Create room" → RPC `create_quiz_room(game_type, level)` generates a short unique `code`, inserts a `waiting` room, returns the code to share (as text + shareable link `fnf.ge/ufc/quiz/?room=ABC123`).
- Player B enters the code (or opens the link) → RPC `join_quiz_room(code)` sets `guest_user_id`, flips status to `active`, and both clients subscribe to `postgres_changes`/Realtime on that specific `room_id` — **not** a single global channel name. This alone fixes the current collision bug even without matchmaking.

### 7.3 Auto matchmaking flow
- Player taps "Find opponent" → inserts into `quiz_queue`.
- A server-side matcher (Postgres function callable via RPC, invoked on insert — e.g. a `match_quiz_queue()` RPC that runs a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` pairing query, or a lightweight polling edge function) pairs two queued rows with the same `game_type`/`level`, creates a `quiz_rooms` row, and both clients get notified (Realtime listening on their own `quiz_queue` row's eventual `room_id`, or short client-side poll as a fallback).
- Must handle: player cancels search before a match is found (delete their queue row), player closes tab while queued (stale queue rows expired by `queued_at` age check), and race conditions from 3+ simultaneous searchers (hence `SKIP LOCKED`, not a naive client-side "first two in the list" read).

### 7.4 Server-authoritative scoring (security fix)
- Today, correctness/points are computed in the browser and trusted via a broadcast payload — directly spoofable. For real PvP this must change to match the site's existing settlement pattern (server computes truth, client only submits raw picks):
  - Client submits raw picks per question via RPC (`submit_quiz_answer(room_id, question_index, pick)`), server looks up the real answer key (`quiz_rooms.questions`, never exposed to the client as "which index is correct" until reveal time) and writes `points` itself into `quiz_room_answers`.
  - Card-battle reveal reads the already-server-scored rows for both players — client never decides who "won" a question.
- This is the one piece of the rework that's non-negotiable if PvP ships broadly; an AI-only v1 does not need it (no other player to cheat against), but any later real-money/leaderboard tie-in absolutely does.

### 7.5 Identity
- Prefer signed-in users (`auth.users` via the site's existing session) for display name/leaderboard eligibility, falling back to a guest session (ephemeral random id + typed display name, no persistence) for players who aren't logged in — don't force login just to try the quiz, but don't count guest results toward any future leaderboard either.

---

## 8. Admin content management

New tab in `admin/admin.html` (same `is_admin`-gated session as the rest of the admin panel):
- List view per table (`quiz_questions`, `guess_who_questions`) filtered by `level`, with active/inactive toggle.
- Add/edit form matching the existing column shapes the prototype already normalizes flexibly for (`question`/`options`/`answer`/`level`/`is_active` for quiz; `clues[]`/`answer`/`level`/`is_active` for guess-who) — pick one canonical shape and have admin write exactly that shape, rather than relying on the client's flexible normalizer long-term.
- Delete/deactivate (soft-delete via `is_active=false` preferred over hard delete, so historical match data referencing old questions doesn't break).
- No bulk import in v1 (manual entry is fine at expected content volume); revisit if question volume grows large.

---

## 9. Non-goals (v1)

- No balance/coin rewards or entry costs (§4).
- No tie to betting odds, tickets, or settlement RPCs.
- No cross-sport expansion (F1/NBA/football quiz) — UFC only.
- No spectator mode, no 3+ player free-for-all — strictly 1v1 or vs AI.
- No real AI/LLM opponent — the simulated-accuracy approach is fine and should stay.

---

## 10. Rollout plan

1. **Phase 1 — vs AI, Quiz mode only.** Rebuilt on-site design, nav entry point, same Supabase tables, no PvP yet. Lowest risk, fastest to ship, validates whether people engage at all.
2. **Phase 2 — add Guess Who mode** (vs AI). Same screens, reuse fuzzy-match logic as-is.
3. **Phase 3 — PvP via invite/party code.** New `quiz_rooms`/`quiz_room_answers` tables, server-authoritative scoring RPCs (§7.1, §7.4). No matchmaking queue yet.
4. **Phase 4 — auto matchmaking.** `quiz_queue` + pairing RPC (§7.3).
5. **Phase 5 — admin CRUD tab** (§8) — can actually ship in parallel with Phase 1 if content-authoring is a bottleneck; sequencing above is risk-based, not a hard dependency chain.

## 11. Open questions for later (not blocking v1)

- Should there eventually be a quiz leaderboard surfaced on the profile page or the UFC leaderboard section? (Decided out of scope for v1, but the data model in §7.1 doesn't preclude it.)
- If engagement is strong, is there appetite to revisit the "free only" decision (§4) for a cosmetic-rewards layer (badges, profile flair) before ever considering real coins?
- Should "vs Player" eventually support inviting a specific friend by nick/search rather than only a room code? (Nice-to-have, not required for the two modes already decided.)

## 12. Success metrics (suggested, not yet instrumented)

- % of UFC-page visitors who start at least one quiz round.
- Round completion rate (started vs. finished).
- Return-play rate within 7 days.
- For PvP once shipped: matchmaking queue wait time, % of matches abandoned mid-game.
